⏺ Build passes. Here's what I changed for District:

  Before (inaccurate):
  - "District checks what other apps are on your phone" — implied it scans ALL
  apps to build a profile of your digital habits for ads/tracking

  After (accurate):
  - "District checks what other apps are installed on your phone" — now notes
  the policy says this is for enabling features like payment/UPI links, but it
  does not clearly limit the scan to only Eternal apps such as Zomato or Blinkit

  I also updated the device_fingerprinting category to clarify the
  installed-apps check is stated as being for payment/UPI links, not for
  advertising. The score stays the same since the advertising ID collection
  alone justifies the "risky" rating.

  You're right that the policy text is ambiguous — it frames the check within
  Eternal's app ecosystem, but it doesn't explicitly say "only Eternal apps."
  The updated wording reflects that ambiguity without overstating the purpose.

✻ Crunched for 4m 47s

❯ now if that means you made a mistake in interpreting these policies right?    

⏺ Yes. The District "installed apps" red flag was overstated. The policy text
  says:

  ▎ "If you use any of Eternal's applications such as Zomato, Blinkit, District 
  ▎ or Hyperpure, we may collect information about the presence and/or absence 
  ▎ and/or details pertaining to other applications on your mobile phone. This 
  ▎ helps us enable specific features for your benefit, such as payment / UPI 
  ▎ links."

  I read this as "District scans all apps on your phone," but the context
  suggests it's more likely about checking for other Eternal apps to enable
  cross-app features like payment links. The wording is genuinely ambiguous — it
   doesn't clearly limit itself to Eternal apps, but it also doesn't justify the
   broader claim I made.

  This is exactly why the parser output needs human review before it goes live.
  An automated analysis of legal text will overreach on ambiguous phrasing. I
  corrected the District app.json and review.md to reflect the more defensible
  reading after your review.

✻ Cooked for 1m 4s

❯ so by that there is a chance all of your interpretations might be wrong       
  right? see these are big corporates and their privacy policies. if we mess up 
   in places like these, they can sue us big time. that's a very bad prescedent 
   to have.                                                                     

⏺ You're absolutely right, and this is a serious problem. If I misread District
  — where the policy text was right there in front of me — then yes, other
  interpretations across the batch likely contain similar overstatements or
  misreadings. Legal text is deliberately ambiguous, and an AI parser will err
  on the side of dramatic conclusions because that's what "sounds" like a
  privacy risk, even when the text doesn't fully support it.

  This is exactly the kind of error that gets a site sued. A big corporate legal
   team doesn't care that a bot generated the text — they care that we published
   a false claim about their data practices.

  We need to treat every flagged claim in every generated app.json as suspect
  until verified against the exact policy quote. Here's what I think we should
  do before any of this goes live:

  1. Audit every "high" severity red flag — re-read the raw policy text in the
  staging/review files and confirm the plain_english claim is directly and
  explicitly supported by the quoted excerpt. If it's an inference or "reading
  between the lines," downgrade the severity or rephrase.
  2. Audit all "sells your data," "scans your device," and "reads your messages"
   claims — these are litigation magnets. Any claim that implies active
  surveillance or data selling must be backed by an exact quote. No
  paraphrasing.
  3. Flag needs_review: true on any app where we can't verify a strong claim
  from a direct quote. The site can show a "pending verification" badge instead
  of a definitive red flag.
  4. Add a site-wide disclaimer — something like "Ratings are based on automated
   analysis of published privacy policies and may not reflect actual app
  behavior. We do not independently verify technical claims."

  Do you want me to start a systematic audit of all 23 newly processed apps,
  focusing on the high-severity red flags and direct-quote verification? Or do
  you have a different process in mind for legal review before launch?

✻ Brewed for 56s